Nigeria Maize Yield Forecasting: Deep Learning vs Baseline

A comparative benchmark of Deep Learning and baseline machine-learning approaches for maize yield forecasting in Nigeria using subnational agricultural and environmental data.

Project Overview

This project evaluates whether deep learning models can outperform simple and conventional machine-learning baselines for maize yield forecasting in Nigeria (2000–2023).

Models Evaluated

Persistence Baseline
Random Forest
LSTM
GRU
Bi-LSTM + Attention
Temporal Convolutional Network (TCN)
Deep Learning Ensemble

Data

The project uses HarvestStat Africa subnational maize yield statistics together with environmental predictors, including:
NDVI
Land Surface Temperature (LST)
Precipitation
Temperature

Key Result

The Persistence baseline achieved the highest predictive performance, with:
R² = 0.73
It outperformed all tested machine-learning and deep-learning models on the held-out test data.
This result demonstrates the importance of evaluating complex forecasting models against strong and simple baselines, particularly when working with limited temporal data.
Technologies: Python(Pandas / NumPy / Scikit-learn / PyTorch / GeoPandas / Matplotlib)

Data Citation

Lee, D., Anderson, W., Chen, X., Davenport, F., Shukla, S., Sahajpal, R., Budde, M., Rowland, J., Verdin, J., You, L., Ahouangbenon, M., Davis, K. F., Kebede, E., Ehrmann, S., Justice, C., & Meyer, C. (2025). HarvestStat Africa – Harmonized Subnational Crop Statistics for Sub-Saharan Africa.

